+# BLOCKED: exact streaming median in O(1) memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median()` to compute the **exact** median of a
+single-pass 200,000-element generator while using **under 64 KB** of peak memory.
+
+Both properties cannot be satisfied simultaneously. This is a fundamental limit,
+not an implementation detail:
+
+- An exact median must identify the two middle order statistics of the input.
+- For a single-pass stream in the comparison model, exact selection of the
+  median provably requires Ω(n) space (Munro & Paterson, *Selection and sorting
+  with limited storage*, 1980). Any algorithm that keeps only O(1) values cannot
+  know which values it discarded belonged on either side of the median.
+- Floating-point values from `random.gauss` are effectively arbitrary distinct
+  reals; there is no bounded summary (count, min/max, moments, fixed-width
+  sketch) from which the exact middle value can be recovered.
+- The input is a one-shot generator, so a second pass or an exact multi-pass
+  selection algorithm is not available either.
+
+This is independent of implementation language: Python's `sorted()` needs
+O(n) memory, and so would any exact streaming algorithm. The test currently
+reports a peak of about 7.2 MB for the 200k-element feed.
+
+We did **not** work around it (e.g. by caching results, re-seeding the known
+generator, spilling to a temp file to fool `tracemalloc`, or hard-coding the
+answer). Those would make the test green without meeting the stated requirement
+and would still OOM the workers on real feeds.
+
+## What is not blocked (done)
+
+- Fixed the reported even-count bug: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` now returns `2.5`
+  instead of `3`. The function returns the mean of the two middle values.
+- All other tests pass: `test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`,
+  `test_generator_input` (4/5 total).
+
+## Options to unblock
+
+Choose one, then the test/requirement should be adjusted accordingly:
+
+1. **Approximate streaming median in O(1) memory** — e.g. the P² algorithm or a
+   t-digest/KLL sketch with a bounded number of centroids. Memory is constant
+   and results have bounded (but not zero) error. Suitable for ingest latency
+   monitoring. Requires changing `test_constant_memory` to allow approximation.
+2. **Exact median with O(n) memory but a smaller constant** — store values in a
+   flat `array('d')` and select with `statistics`/quickselect. Roughly 1.6 MB
+   for 200k samples (not 64 KB), still O(n). Requires relaxing the memory bound.
+3. **Exact, bounded memory via external storage** — spill to disk and perform an
+   external selection/sort. Bounded RAM but O(n) disk and much higher latency;
+   usually the wrong trade-off for ingest workers.
+4. **Two passes / indexable input** — if the feed can be replayed, an exact
+   multi-pass selection algorithm needs O(1) memory. Requires changing the input
+   contract from a one-shot iterable to a replayable source.
